<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberators' civil war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/44-bce">44</a>–<a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberators</a>' civil war was the Roman civil war that followed the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 44 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, Octavian and Lepidus fought the assassins, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a>, who styled themselves liberators. It ended with the triumvirs' victory at Philippi in Macedonia in October 42 BCE, where Brutus and Cassius killed themselves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberators-civil-war.jpg" alt="Liberators' civil war" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vikarna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Philippi_campaign_42_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
